namespace DGtal {
//----------------------------------------
/*!
@page modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators Estimators based on maximal segments
@writers Tristan Roussillon

[TOC]

Part of the \ref packageGeometry.

This part of the manual describes how to estimate local geometric
quantities (tangent or normal vectors, curvature...) along a digital curve
from the maximal segments of a given primitive (digital straight segments,
digital circular arcs...).

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Overview Overview

The estimation relies on three kinds of objects:

- a segment computer (see \ref moduleGridCurveAnalysis), which recognizes
  the segments of a given primitive along a range of elements (points or
  steps of a digital curve), e.g. ArithmeticalDSSComputer (digital
  straight segments on points), ArithmeticalDSSComputerOnArrows (digital
  straight segments on steps, i.e. arrows), StabbingLineComputer (straight
  segments on steps, i.e. pairs of inner and outer points),
  StabbingCircleComputer (digital circular arcs, DCAs, on steps);

- a segment computer estimator (model of concepts::CSegmentComputerEstimator), which
  estimates a quantity at an element from one segment, e.g.
  TangentFromDSSEstimator, TangentAngleFromDSSEstimator,
  CurvatureFromDSSEstimator, CurvatureFromDSSLengthEstimator,
  NormalFromStabbingLineEstimator, NormalFromDCAEstimator, TangentFromDCAEstimator, CurvatureFromDCAEstimator
  (see SegmentComputerEstimators.h);

- an estimator (model of concepts::CCurveLocalGeometricEstimator), which combines
  the maximal segments passing through each element of the curve, computed
  by a saturated segmentation (SaturatedSegmentation):
  MostCenteredMaximalSegmentEstimator or LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator.

Both estimators are used in the same way: they are constructed from a
segment computer and a segment computer estimator, initialized with the
whole range of elements (iterators for an open curve, circulators for a
closed curve), and then evaluated either at one element or on a subrange:

@code
typedef ArithmeticalDSSComputer<ConstIterator, int, 4> SegmentComputer;
typedef TangentFromDSSEstimator<SegmentComputer> SCEstimator;
typedef LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator<SegmentComputer, SCEstimator> Estimator;

Estimator estimator( SegmentComputer(), SCEstimator() );
estimator.init( itb, ite );
// estimation at one element
auto tangent = estimator.eval( it, h );
// estimation on a (sub)range
std::vector<Estimator::Quantity> tangents;
estimator.eval( itb, ite, std::back_inserter( tangents ), h );
@endcode

The evaluation on a (sub)range is faster than the evaluation at each
element, since the maximal segments are computed only once.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-MostCentered Most centered maximal segment estimator

MostCenteredMaximalSegmentEstimator assigns to each element the quantity
estimated from one maximal segment passing through it, which is called
the most centered maximal segment (see mostCenteredMaximalSegment()).
It is not exactly the most centered one, but the maximal segment
containing the tangent at this element defined by Feschet and Tougne
\cite FeschetTougneDGCI1999 (definition 3): the symmetric tangent at an
element \f$ i \f$ is the segment \f$ [i-k, i+k] \f$ such that
\f$ [i-k-1, i+k+1] \f$ is not a segment, and it is extended as much as
possible at the front, then at the back. Since several maximal segments
may contain the symmetric tangent, the one with the largest index is
chosen.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Lambda Lambda-maximal segment estimator

LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator assigns to each element a combination of
the quantities estimated from all the maximal segments passing through it
(\f$\lambda\f$-MST estimator \cite LachaudIVC2007). Let \f$ M_i \f$ be
the maximal segments passing through an element \f$ k \f$,
\f$ q_i(k) \f$ the quantity estimated at \f$ k \f$ from \f$ M_i \f$, and
\f$ e_i(k) \f$ the eccentricity of \f$ k \f$ in \f$ M_i \f$, i.e. its
relative position between the extremities of \f$ M_i \f$. The estimated
quantity at \f$ k \f$ is
\f[ \frac{\sum_i \lambda(e_i(k)) q_i(k)}{\sum_i \lambda(e_i(k))}, \f]
where \f$ \lambda \f$ is a mapping from \f$ [0,1] \f$ to \f$ \mathbb{R}^+ \f$
with \f$ \lambda(0) = \lambda(1) = 0 \f$ and \f$ \lambda > 0 \f$ elsewhere,
e.g. functors::Lambda64Function (default), functors::LambdaSinFromPiFunction,
functors::LambdaExponentialFunction or functors::LambdaTriangleFunction
(see FunctorsLambdaMST.h).

The eccentricity of an element only depends on its index in the segment
and on the number of elements of the segment. It is given by a functor,
which depends on the nature of the elements:
- functors::PointEccentricity (default) if the elements are points, as in
  \cite LachaudIVC2007 (e.g. the points of a GridCurve, used by
  ArithmeticalDSSComputer): the element of index \f$ i \f$ in a segment of
  \f$ n \f$ elements has eccentricity \f$ i/(n-1) \f$;
- functors::StepEccentricity if the elements are steps (e.g. the arrows of
  a GridCurve, used by ArithmeticalDSSComputerOnArrows, or its pairs of
  inner and outer points, used by StabbingLineComputer and
  StabbingCircleComputer): the element of index \f$ i \f$ in a segment of
  \f$ n \f$ elements has eccentricity \f$ (i + 1/2)/n \f$.

@code
typedef StabbingCircleComputer<ConstCirculator> SegmentComputer;
typedef LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator<SegmentComputer, SCEstimator,
  functors::LambdaTriangleFunction, functors::StepEccentricity> Estimator;
@endcode

Since the estimated quantities are averaged, they must form a vector space
(e.g. the unit vectors of TangentFromDSSEstimator) and be consistently
oriented along the curve, otherwise opposite vectors cancel out. For
instance, the normal vectors of NormalFromDCAEstimator always point toward
the outside of the curve, i.e. from the inner points toward the outer
points of the steps, whatever the convexity of the DCAs (the normal vector
of a DCA points away from the center of its separating circle on convex
parts but toward it on concave parts).

@note LambdaMST2D is another implementation of the \f$\lambda\f$-MST
estimator, which takes a segmentation and evaluates the tangent at a given
point.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Benchmark Comparison

The benchmark normalEstimators-benchmark.cpp studies the multigrid
convergence and the running times of both estimators for the normal
vectors computed, at the steps of the curve, from the maximal DSSs of
ArithmeticalDSSComputerOnArrows, from the maximal segments of
StabbingLineComputer and from the maximal DCAs of StabbingCircleComputer,
along the boundaries of a digitized ellipse and of a digitized flower, for
grid steps \f$ h \f$ from 1 to 0.0005. The normal vectors are given by
NormalFromStabbingLineEstimator and NormalFromDCAEstimator, and by the
tangent vectors of TangentFromDSSEstimator rotated by a right angle. The
error is the angle between the estimated and the true normal directions.

All the estimators converge. The mean angular error of all the estimators
is bounded by \f$ C h^{2/3} \f$ and their maximal error by
\f$ C' h^{1/3} \f$, where the constants \f$ C \f$ and \f$ C' \f$
(shown on the graphs) are the smallest ones bounding all the measures
(rounded up). The errors of the estimators based on straight segments
decrease roughly at these rates, whereas the errors of the estimators
based on circular arcs decrease faster (roughly as \f$ h \f$ for the mean
error). The \f$\lambda\f$-MST estimators are 1.5 to 2.5 times more
accurate than the most centered ones, for a running time 1.1 to 2.5 times
longer. The estimators based on circular arcs are the most accurate ones,
but they are about 20 to 40 times slower than the estimators based on
DSSs.

The \f$\lambda\f$-MST estimator based on StabbingLineComputer is more
accurate than the one based on DSSs, although both work on the steps of
the curve, because the segments are not the same.
A sequence of steps is a segment of StabbingLineComputer if there is a
straight line separating the inner and outer pixel centers of its steps,
the pixel centers lying on the straight line being allowed on both sides
(the preimage computed by Preimage2D is closed). A sequence of steps is a
standard DSS (ArithmeticalDSSComputerOnArrows) if and only if the same
holds, but with the pixel centers lying on the straight line allowed on
one side only (\f$ \mu \leq ax - by < \mu + \omega \f$). The segments of
StabbingLineComputer thus form a larger class: since the straight lines
passing through pixel centers are very frequent on a grid, its maximal
segments are generally longer and more numerous than the maximal DSSs of
the same curve, and do not have the same extremities. Moreover, the
straight lines used to estimate the normal vectors are not defined in the
same way: the normal vector of a DSS is orthogonal to its leaning lines,
of direction \f$ (b,a) \f$, whereas the straight line returned by
StabbingLineComputer::getParameters is the bisector of the two straight
lines of the preimage of extreme slopes, i.e. a straight line in the middle
of the preimage (see Preimage2D::getSeparatingStraightLine). Even for the
same range of steps, the two normal vectors thus differ slightly.

\image html normals-convergence-ellipse.png "Mean angular error (radian) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized ellipse as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html normals-max-error-ellipse.png "Maximal angular error (radian) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized ellipse as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html normals-convergence-flower.png "Mean angular error (radian) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized flower as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html normals-max-error-flower.png "Maximal angular error (radian) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized flower as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html normals-timings.png "Running times (ms) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized flower as a function of the number of points." width=70%

\subsection modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Length Length estimation

The length of a curve may be estimated by integration of the estimated
normal vectors: each step of the curve, of length \f$ h \f$, contributes
\f$ h |n \cdot u| \f$, where \f$ n \f$ is the estimated unit normal vector
at the step and \f$ u \f$ the trivial normal vector of the step. The
benchmark compares the length estimated from each of the normal estimators
with the length of the boundary of the shape. The relative error is
bounded by \f$ C h \f$ for all the normal estimators (the constant
\f$ C \f$, shown on the graphs, being the smallest one bounding all the
measures, rounded up). It decreases more quickly for the
\f$\lambda\f$-MST estimators than for the most centered ones, and much more
quickly for the estimators based on circular arcs (about \f$ 10^{-7} \f$
for \f$ h = 0.0005 \f$).

\image html length-convergence-ellipse.png "Relative error of the length of the boundary of a digitized ellipse, estimated by integration of the normal vectors, as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html length-convergence-flower.png "Relative error of the length of the boundary of a digitized flower, estimated by integration of the normal vectors, as a function of the grid step h." width=70%

\subsection modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Curvature Curvature estimation

The curvature may be estimated from the maximal segments of
StabbingCircleComputer with CurvatureFromDCAEstimator
\cite RoussillonLachaudIWCIA2011. With MostCenteredMaximalSegmentEstimator,
it is the MDCA (most centered digital circular arc) curvature estimator,
whose multigrid convergence is studied in \cite SchindeleJMIV2017. Its absolute value
is the inverse of the radius of the separating circle, and its sign depends
on the convexity of the DCA (see StabbingCircleComputer::isConvex()) and on
the orientation of the scanning, given as a template parameter: for a curve
scanned in a counter-clockwise orientation, the curvature is positive on
convex parts and negative on concave parts. The benchmark
compares the curvature estimated with both estimators with the true
curvature of the shape. All the absolute errors, mean and maximal, are
bounded by \f$ C h^{1/3} \f$ (the constant \f$ C \f$, shown on the
graphs, being the smallest one bounding all the measures, rounded up).
The mean absolute error decreases faster, slightly faster for the
\f$\lambda\f$-MST estimator, whereas the maximal absolute error is close
to the bound, especially along the non-convex shape.

\image html curvature-convergence-ellipse.png "Mean and maximal absolute errors of the curvature estimated from DCAs along the boundary of a digitized ellipse as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
\image html curvature-convergence-flower.png "Mean and maximal absolute errors of the curvature estimated from DCAs along the boundary of a digitized flower as a function of the grid step h." width=70%

*/

}
